<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<chapter xml:id="ch5-chapter-ht-1p-evaluate" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Evaluating Hypotheses</title>

  <introduction>
    <p> This chapter formally covers the evaluate step of the model-simulate-evaluate process used
        for simulation-based hypothesis testing. Students will learn about p-values and the standardized value of the statistic/Z-score/test statistic. Students will also learn the difference between a one-sided and a two-sided test. The chapter ends with a discussion on significance, discernability, and decision errors.
      </p>
  </introduction>


  <objectives>
    <ol>
    <li>Numerically measure the strength of evidence against the null hypothesis using standardized statistics (Z-scores) and p-values.</li>

    <li>Interpret standardized statistics and p-values, demonstrating an understanding of what these measures can and can not tell you. </li>

    <li>Use Z-scores and p-values to make decisions concerning hypothesis tests.</li>
  
    <li>Perform both one-sided and two-sided tests, understanding the differences between them.</li>
    
    <li>Understand the types of errors that can occur with hypothesis testing, their importance, and how they likelihood is related to the discernability level.</li>

  </ol>
  </objectives>

  
